  "preamble": "Spirantization of the aspirated velar stop [kʰ] to the velar fricative [x], usually together with the other members of an aspirated series. Greek χ, an aspirate [kʰ] in Classical Attic, had become a fricative by about the 4th century AD (χρόνος /kʰró.nos/ > /ˈxro.nos/), and Proto-Indo-Iranian *kʰ became Proto-Iranian *x (Sanskrit sákhā vs. Avestan haxa 'friend', Sanskrit khara vs. Persian xar 'donkey').",
  "phoneticEffects": "Spirantization, Lenition",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Greek (Attic > Koine)",
      "languageFamily": "Hellenic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Attic χ [kʰ], e.g. χώρα 'land'",
          "to": "[x] (Modern Greek χώρα [ˈxora]); [ç] before front vowels, χείρ /kʰěːr/ > /ˈçir/",
          "note": "Rome catacomb inscriptions of the 2nd–3rd centuries still point to /kʰ/; Codex Vaticanus (4th century) gives strong evidence for fricative χ."
        },
        {
          "from": "Attic χρόνος /kʰró.nos/ 'time'",
          "to": "4th-century Koine /ˈxro.nos/ (Modern Greek /ˈxɾo.nos/)"
        },
        {
          "from": "Attic χρυσός /kʰryː.sós/ 'gold'",
          "to": "4th-century Koine /xryˈsos/ (Modern Greek /xɾiˈsos/)"
        },
        {
          "from": "Attic χαρά /kʰa.rǎː/ 'joy'",
          "to": "4th-century Koine /xaˈra/ (Modern Greek /xaˈɾa/)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Avestan and Persian (Proto-Iranian)",
      "languageFamily": "Iranian",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Indo-Iranian *sákʰā 'friend' (Sanskrit sákhā)",
          "to": "Proto-Iranian *háxā > Avestan haxa",
          "note": "The Proto-Indo-Iranian voiceless aspirates *pʰ, *tʰ, *kʰ were spirantized to *f, *θ, *x in most Iranian languages."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Indo-Iranian *kʰáras 'donkey' (Sanskrit khara)",
